Author: Danny Milosavljevic <dannym@friendly-machines.com>
Date: 2026-04-20
Subject: Depack `treference' and `tlocation' for the tp2cc C++ bootstrap
License: expat

These two records carry no file-format role -- they are internal
codegen/operand state for the i386 backend, not written to PPU files
or to disk in any other form.  Upstream declared them `packed' only
to save RAM on 1990s-era builds.

tp2cc translates Pascal `packed record' to `struct [[gnu::packed]]' in
C++.  Unlike `#pragma pack(push,1)', the attribute form makes gcc
reject binding a C++ reference to a misaligned member, which is
exactly what a Pascal `var' parameter pointing at
`treference.offset' or `tlocation.register' becomes under the C++
lowering: gcc diagnoses `cannot bind packed field X to T&' at
compile time.

On native x86 the original code worked because the CPU tolerates
unaligned loads; in C++ the resulting references are undefined
regardless of hardware.  Depacking these two in-memory records
restores natural alignment without disturbing any on-disk layout,
and leaves `[[gnu::packed]]' enforced for records that really are
file formats (`ppuheader', `tsymbol', `tarhdr', ...).

--- a/compiler/i386/cpubase.pas
+++ b/compiler/i386/cpubase.pas
@@ -455,7 +455,11 @@

   { immediate/reference record }
   preference = ^treference;
-  treference = packed record
+  { was `packed record` -- depacked for the tp2cc C++ bootstrap. This record
+    is in-memory-only (no file-format role), and packing it forced misaligned
+    access to its `longint`/pointer fields. C++ references to those fields
+    are UB when misaligned, which `[[gnu::packed]]` rightly rejects. }
+  treference = record
      is_immediate : boolean; { is this used as reference or immediate }
      segment,
      base,
@@ -507,7 +511,8 @@
   );

   plocation = ^tlocation;
-  tlocation = packed record
+  { depacked for tp2cc bootstrap, see note on `treference` above. }
+  tlocation = record
      case loc : tloc of
         LOC_MEM,LOC_REFERENCE : (reference : treference);
         LOC_FPU : ();
